Certainly! Below is a complex JavaScript program that demonstrates the use of advanced features such as asynchronous functions, Promises, arrow functions, the spread operator, and ES6 classes.

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
  constructor(url) {
    this.url = url;
  }

  async fetchData() {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(this.url);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
      const data = await response.json();
      return data;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Fetching error:', error);
      throw error;
    }
  }
}

class DataProcessor {
  static process(data) {
    return data.map(({ id, name, age }) => ({
      id,
      name,
      age: age + 1,
    }));
  }

  static filter(data, predicate) {
    return data.filter(predicate);
  }
}

const fetchDataAndProcess = async (url) => {
  const fetcher = new DataFetcher(url);
  try {
    const data = await fetcher.fetchData();
    const processedData = DataProcessor.process(data);
    const filteredData = DataProcessor.filter(processedData, ({ age }) => age > 30);
    console.log('Filtered Data:', filteredData);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Processing error:', error);
  }
};

const url = 'https://api.example.com/data';
fetchDataAndProcess(url);
```

This program defines two classes: `DataFetcher` for fetching data from a given URL and `DataProcessor` for processing and filtering the fetched data. The `fetchDataAndProcess` function demonstrates asynchronous operations, error handling, and data manipulation.